# dns.manifest — config owned by mod_dns (systemd-resolved + DoT).
#
# Layered (update-system.md phase 3):
#   • /usr/share/w/defaults/dns.conf   — W's resolver catalog + default selection.
#     Class managed: every update overwrites it, which is exactly how a new
#     resolver or an improved default reaches an installed machine. Catalog entries
#     MERGE per key, so a DNS_<name> the admin adds below coexists with these.
#   • /usr/share/w/defaults/dns.schema — who may set each key (all system-scope:
#     one resolver serves the whole machine and DoT strictness is security policy).
#   • /etc/w/dns.conf — this machine's DEVIATIONS only. Class override: the update
#     never touches it; `w-reset dns` drops them back to the vendor layer.
#
# The rendered /etc/systemd/resolved.conf.d/10-w-dns.conf is w-dns output
# (regenerate with `sudo w-dns apply`), not tracked here. The NetworkManager and
# mDNS drop-ins are static W-owned system files, and so is the sysctl drop-in that
# turns client-side TCP Fast Open off for resolved's DoT streams (deliberately NOT
# in w-kernel's hardening profile: `harden off` must not bring the DNS hang back).
#
# Format: <path>\t<class>   class: managed | override
/usr/share/w/defaults/dns.conf	managed
/usr/share/w/defaults/dns.schema	managed
/etc/w/dns.conf	override
/etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/10-w-dns.conf	managed
/etc/systemd/resolved.conf.d/20-w-mdns.conf	managed
/etc/sysctl.d/80-w-dns.conf	managed
